.TH UNIQ 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
uniq \- report or omit repeated lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniq
.RB [ \-cduiz ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR n ]
.RI [ file
.RI [ outfile ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uniq
reads lines from
.I file
and writes each run of adjacent equal lines as one line, the first of the
run.
It does not find repeats that are not adjacent, so sort the input first,
as in
.BR "sort file | uniq" .
Options write only the repeated lines or only the unique ones, count the
lines in each run, and change which lines are equal.
This
.B uniq
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
.B uniq
reads standard input.
With no
.IR outfile ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
it writes standard output.
A third operand is an error.
.I outfile
is created if it does not exist, and truncated when it is opened, which
is before any input is read.
Naming the input file as
.I outfile
empties it and writes nothing.
.PP
A line ends at an LF, and the last line of the input need not have one.
A CR directly before an LF is removed.
A NUL byte also ends a line, and is not part of it.
.PP
Two lines are equal when the parts of them that are compared are equal as
bytes.
The part compared is what remains of a line after the first
.B \-f
fields and then the first
.B \-s
bytes are skipped, cut off after
.B \-w
bytes.
With
.BR \-i ,
ASCII letters are equal to their other case.
.PP
Lines are written in input order.
Each line written is the first line of its run, as read except for a
removed CR, and ends with an LF, or a NUL with
.BR \-z ,
even when the last line of the input had no LF.
By default one line is written for every run.
With
.BR \-d ,
only runs of two or more lines are written, and with
.BR \-u ,
only runs of one line.
With
.BR \-c ,
each line written is preceded by the number of lines in its run, as a
decimal number right-aligned in 7 columns, and one space.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cd ,
and may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-f1 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
The numbers are decimal, from 0 to 2147483647.
A sign, a
.B 0x
prefix, leading white space, anything after the digits, or a larger
value is an error:
.B uniq
prints a message and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-c
Precede each line written with the number of lines in its run: the
count in decimal, right-aligned in 7 columns, then a space.
A count of more than 7 digits widens the field.
.TP
.B \-d
Write only the first line of each run of two or more lines.
With
.BR \-u ,
nothing is written, and the exit status is still 0.
.TP
.B \-u
Write only the lines that are not repeated: the runs of one line.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore case when comparing lines.
Only ASCII letters are folded, so other letters must match byte for
byte.
.TP
.B \-z
End each line written with a NUL byte instead of an LF.
It does not change how input is read: input lines end at an LF or a NUL
either way.
.TP
.BI "\-f " n
Skip the first
.I n
fields of each line before comparing.
A field is any white space, possibly none, followed by a run of other
characters, and white space is a space, tab, VT, FF, or CR.
The white space after the last skipped field is compared.
A line with fewer than
.I n
fields has nothing left to compare.
.TP
.BI "\-s " n
After skipping any fields, skip the first
.I n
bytes of what remains, or fewer if the line ends first.
The unit is a byte, so a multibyte UTF-8 character can be split.
.TP
.BI "\-w " n
Compare at most
.I n
bytes of the part left after
.B \-f
and
.BR \-s .
The default compares all of it.
With 0 every line equals the one before, so all of the input is one
run.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and elsewhere is an unknown
option.
.PP
Any other option, such as
.BR \-h ,
or an option without its argument, prints an error and the usage
message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so that a later argument
such as
.B \-c
is a file name.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B uniq
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a missing option argument, or more than two operands,
with a usage message on standard error; an invalid number; a file could
not be opened; a read or write error; or memory ran out.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count how many times each word appears, most frequent first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort words.txt | uniq \-c | sort \-rn
      3 pear
      2 apple
      1 fig
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the words that appear more than once, and the words that appear
only once:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort words.txt | uniq \-d
apple
pear
$ sort words.txt | uniq \-u
fig
.EE
.RE
.PP
Collapse runs of log lines that differ only in the time stamp, the
first field:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat log.txt
10:01 start
10:02 start
10:03 stop
10:04 stop
10:05 start
$ uniq \-f 1 log.txt
10:01 start
10:03 stop
10:05 start
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compare only the first three bytes of each line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqabc1\enabc2\enabd1\en\(aq | uniq \-c \-w 3
      2 abc1
      1 abd1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read a file, and write the result to another:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort in.txt | uniq \- out.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR uniq :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
and no
.BR \-D .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-z
changes only the end of the lines written, and an LF still ends an
input line.
.IP \(bu 2
A CR directly before an LF is removed, and a NUL byte ends a line.
.IP \(bu 2
The count of
.B \-c
is 32 bits, in the 64-bit kit too, so a run of more than 4294967295
lines wraps it.
.PP
Lines may be of any length.
.B uniq
holds the line being compared and the line before it in memory.
An empty file writes nothing.
.PP
Files and standard input and output are binary, so Ctrl-Z is not the end
of a file and CR LF line endings are not translated.
Input CR LF becomes LF in the output.
A CR anywhere else in a line is kept, and counts as white space for
.BR \-f .
To write CR LF line endings, pass the output through
.BR unix2dos (1).
.PP
File names may use drive letters, backslashes, and forward slashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names that busybox accepts, such as
.I /dev/null
for either operand and
.I /dev/stdout
for
.IR outfile ,
work as operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.I /dev/stdin
and
.I /dev/fd/0
fail as
.IR file ,
because
.B uniq
closes its standard input to open
.I file
in its place; give
.B \-
instead.
A directory fails as
.I file
with
.BR "Permission denied" ,
and as
.I outfile
with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
To end input typed at a console, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and
press Enter.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
to the console's code page, unless the first of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
that is set is
.BR C .
Escape sequences in the input, such as those for color, take effect.
With
.BR \-z ,
a console receives no line terminators, because the console writer stops
at the NUL, so the lines run together.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR comm (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
